WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:04.879
In this section, we're going to discuss the various types of cameras available in

2
00:00:04.879 --> 00:00:06.360
Unreal Engine.

3
00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:11.240
When dealing with cameras in Unreal, we typically refer to two specific types, Game Camera

4
00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:14.720
Actors and Cine Camera Actors.

5
00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:19.100
Game cameras are the most basic type of camera and have limited functionality.

6
00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:23.600
Game cameras are often used as the primary player camera in video games due to the

7
00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:26.079
lightweight nature of their design.

8
00:00:26.079 --> 00:00:30.739
Cine cameras have a more robust set of features, including sensor crop, film back,

9
00:00:30.739 --> 00:00:32.780
depth of field, and tracking.

10
00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:36.619
They are often used for high-end cinematics and projects that want to achieve a more

11
00:00:36.619 --> 00:00:38.259
filmic quality.

12
00:00:38.259 --> 00:00:42.020
It's important to note that while games typically use game cameras for the in-game

13
00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:47.460
player camera, utilizing cine cameras in sequences is perfectly acceptable and often

14
00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:48.619
the norm.

15
00:00:48.619 --> 00:00:52.880
Outside of the performance costs associated with cine cameras, there's nothing preventing

16
00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:57.219
you from using either game or cine cameras at runtime.

17
00:00:57.219 --> 00:01:01.899
Just note that cine cameras offer a more robust set of features over game cameras

18
00:01:01.899 --> 00:01:05.500
and carry a certain performance cost for those features.

19
00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:10.180
You can access the Cine Camera Actor from the Place Actors tab in the editor.

20
00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:14.379
Within the Cine Camera Actor's details, you will find all the settings associated

21
00:01:14.379 --> 00:01:20.620
with Look At, Tracking, Film Back or Sensor Settings, Lens Settings, and Post-Processing

22
00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:21.879
Settings.

23
00:01:21.879 --> 00:01:25.799
For the purposes of this class, you are free to experiment with any and all of these

24
00:01:25.799 --> 00:01:26.799
settings.

25
00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:32.340
One particular setting to take note of is the Constrain Aspect Ratio.

26
00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:37.299
If this is turned on, the camera will respect the aspect ratio of the camera as opposed

27
00:01:37.299 --> 00:01:40.200
to the player's monitor resolution.

28
00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:44.280
If players are using a different aspect ratio for their monitor than that of the

29
00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:50.829
cine camera specifies, black bars will appear on the top or sides of the viewport.

30
00:01:50.829 --> 00:01:54.870
Disabled in this checkbox will allow the camera to adjust its aspect ratio to the

31
00:01:54.909 --> 00:01:57.250
ratio of the player's monitor.

32
00:01:57.250 --> 00:02:01.469
Be sure to test the sequence on the target hardware in the event of unwanted scaling

33
00:02:01.469 --> 00:02:03.909
or distortion.

34
00:02:03.909 --> 00:02:09.949
When placing cameras into a sequence, a Camera Cuts track is automatically created.

35
00:02:09.949 --> 00:02:14.469
This is how the sequence associates the camera with the final output.

36
00:02:14.469 --> 00:02:18.509
You can change the camera binding at any time by right clicking on the camera cut in

37
00:02:18.509 --> 00:02:22.229
the timeline and selecting the Camera Binding ID.

38
00:02:22.229 --> 00:02:27.870
Note, you can have multiple cameras nested in a sequence and swap between them by changing

39
00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:30.659
the Camera Binding ID.

40
00:02:30.659 --> 00:02:35.340
A few other components worth mentioning are the Scene Capture Component 2D camera,

41
00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:39.340
which captures the scene and can output to a rendered target texture.

42
00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:44.500
V-Cam Actors, which is used to transmit camera data to a device such as an iPad through

43
00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:46.139
the Live Link plugin.

44
00:02:46.139 --> 00:02:50.620
And Lens Component, which is used to create calibrated lens files to match a real world

45
00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:51.620
equivalent.
